course: dictionary
lesson: 8774
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Shadow is a word with more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it, because they are its context. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence.
explain: «The Zebra and the Giraffe step into the striped and blotchy shadows.» Here shadows are areas of darkness, places where light does not reach. The word stepping into them is a clue.
explain: «Behind one drawn blind they see the shadow of a bird-cage.» Here a shadow is a dark shape on a surface, made because something blocks the light. The bird-cage is the thing blocking it.
explain: Shadow can also be a verb. If a trainee chef shadows a head chef, she follows her closely to learn the job. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check: does it still make sense?
fact: A shadow is a dark shape made on a surface when something blocks the light.
fact: Light travels in straight lines, so a shadow is always on the side away from the light.
fact: «Shadows are longest when the Sun is low and shortest when it is highest in the sky.»
fact: «Without a shadow of a doubt» means completely sure, and «afraid of your own shadow» means very timid.
fact: To shadow someone is to follow them closely, often to learn their job.
exercise: «The Zebra and the Giraffe step into the striped and blotchy shadows.» In this sentence, shadows means | Friends who follow them everywhere | *Areas of darkness where light does not reach | Shapes drawn with a pencil
exercise: Mia wants to be a vet, so she will shadow Dr Patel for a week. What does shadow mean here? | To hide from the light | To draw a dark shape | To blow out a candle | *To follow someone closely to learn their job
exercise: Shadows are longest when the Sun is | High in the sky | Behind a cloud | *Low in the sky | Hidden by the moon
test:
Which phrase means completely sure? | *Without a shadow of a doubt | Afraid of your own shadow | In the shadow of the hill
Light travels in straight lines, so where is a shadow found? | *On the side away from the light | On the side nearest the light | Only at midnight
exam:
«Behind one drawn blind they see the shadow of a bird-cage and a little bird who wakes, yawns and tucks his head back in.» In this sentence, shadow means | *A dark shape made when something blocks the light | To follow someone closely | A shade of colour darker than black | A tint made by adding white
